[manpage_begin admin-fail2ban n [vset version]]
[moddesc {NaviServer Manual}]
[titledesc {Protecting NaviServer with Fail2Ban}]

[description]


[uri https://github.com/fail2ban/fail2ban/wiki Fail2Ban] monitors log files
for repeated events, such as failed logins or known probes, and temporarily
blocks the source IP addresses.

This guide describes an optional integration with Fail2Ban on Linux.
The guide described Configure your application to log known probe requests. Fail2Ban watches
for those log entries and applies temporary bans at the host firewall.
Ordinary 404 responses and normal crawling or harvesting should not
contribute to the ban count.

[section {Emit a probe marker in NaviServer}]

From a request handler or filter, emit this line only after your application
has classified the request as a probe:

[example {
ns_log Notice "F2B_NAVISERVER_PROBE ip=[ns_conn peeraddr -source direct]"
}]

[para]

Keep the message fixed apart from the IP address. Do not include URLs,
headers, or other client-supplied text. Use the direct peer address when
NaviServer receives the client connection directly, including Docker
forwarding that preserves the source address. Behind a reverse proxy, the
direct peer may be the proxy itself. In that case, establish the client
address through a trusted proxy configuration and enforce the ban where
packets still carry that source address. An application log can name a
client that the firewall on the backend host cannot see.

[section {Install the filter}]

In [file /etc/fail2ban/filter.d/naviserver-probes.conf], install [emph one] of
the following versions. For the standard installation under
[const {/usr/local/ns}], the default system log is
[file /usr/local/ns/log/nsd.log]. If NaviServer writes elsewhere, replace
that path in both commands below and in the jail's [const logpath] setting.
For Docker, use the path as seen from the host. Check the actual bytes in
the log to choose the filter:

[example {
 head -n 3 /usr/local/ns/log/nsd.log | sed -n l
}]

[para]

These examples expect NaviServer's single-line layout: a timestamp with
second precision, then two bracketed fields (process/thread ID and thread
name), then the severity and message. Logs with microseconds, relative
timestamps, a different prefix, or expanded messages need a corresponding
change. If logging deduplicates identical messages, verify that repeated
probe events still produce distinct marker lines.

[subsection {Filter Without ANSI colors}]

Example line:

[example {
[19/Nov/2026:10:23:43][7.37987][-nscp:1-] Notice: F2B_NAVISERVER_PROBE ip=192.0.2.10
}]

[para]

Filter:

[example {
[Definition]
datepattern = ^\[%%d/%%b/%%Y:%%H:%%M:%%S\]
failregex = ^\[[^\]\r\n]+\]\[[^\]\r\n]+\] Notice: F2B_NAVISERVER_PROBE ip=<ADDR>$
}]

[subsection {Filter With ANSI colors}]

In the colored NaviServer format used on OpenACS, an ANSI color sequence
precedes the timestamp. The prefix color is reset after the space following
the thread name, a second color sequence precedes the severity, and a final
reset ends the message. A byte-escaped example (the actual log contains ESC
bytes, not the string [const {\x1b}]):

[example {
\x1b[0;32m[19/Nov/2026:10:23:43][7.37987][-nscp:1-] \x1b[0m\x1b[0;39mNotice: F2B_NAVISERVER_PROBE ip=192.0.2.10\x1b[0m
}]

[para]

Filter:

[example {
[Definition]
datepattern = ^\x1b\[[0-9]+;[0-9]+m\[%%d/%%b/%%Y:%%H:%%M:%%S\]
failregex = ^\[[^\]\r\n]+\]\[[^\]\r\n]+\] \x1b\[0m\x1b\[[0-9]+;[0-9]+mNotice: F2B_NAVISERVER_PROBE ip=<ADDR>\x1b\[0m$
}]

[para]

In both variants, Fail2Ban extracts and removes the timestamp before
applying the anchored [const {failregex}]. The colored date pattern also
consumes the initial color sequence so the remaining line starts at the
process/thread field. The doubled percent signs are required in the
configuration file. Fail2Ban's [const {<ADDR>}] tag matches IPv4 and IPv6
literals.

[para]

Test against the real logfile before enabling the jail:

[example {
fail2ban-regex /usr/local/ns/log/nsd.log /etc/fail2ban/filter.d/naviserver-probes.conf --print-all-matched
}]

[para]

Check that a known marker matches and unrelated lines do not. If you see no
date matches, inspect the raw log prefix again. If dates match but a marker
does not, check the bracketed fields, spacing, severity, and the trailing
color reset.

[section {Configure the jail on the host}]

Create [file /etc/fail2ban/jail.d/naviserver-probes.local]:

[example {
[naviserver-probes]
enabled  = false
filter   = naviserver-probes
backend  = auto
logpath  = /usr/local/ns/log/nsd.log tail
usedns   = no
findtime = 10m
maxretry = 3
bantime  = 15m
}]

[para]

Change [const {logpath}] if your logfile is elsewhere. Use an absolute path
readable by the host's Fail2Ban process. The [const {tail}] modifier starts
a newly watched file at its end. Three matching entries from the same source
within ten minutes trigger a fifteen-minute ban with these settings.

[para]

Add one of the following action blocks under the same jail section. Both
examples use the packaged [const {iptables-multiport}] action. Separate
action and chain names let TCP and UDP use different port lists.

[subsection {NaviServer listening directly on the host}]

Use [const {INPUT}] and the actual listener ports. For HTTP on TCP/80, HTTPS
on TCP/443, and HTTP/3 on UDP/443:

[example {
action = iptables-multiport[actname=naviserver-tcp, name=naviserver-tcp, chain=INPUT, port="80,443", protocol=tcp]
         iptables-multiport[actname=naviserver-udp, name=naviserver-udp, chain=INPUT, port=443, protocol=udp]
}]

[para]

Omit the UDP action if you do not serve HTTP/3. This also applies to Docker
host networking when NaviServer listens directly in the host's network
namespace.

[subsection {NaviServer in Docker with bridge networking}]

Run Fail2Ban on the host. Give the host access to NaviServer's system
logfile through a bind mount or another host-readable path. Set
[const logpath] to that path. For example, if you expose the logfile under
[file /srv/naviserver/log] on the host:

[example {
logpath = /srv/naviserver/log/nsd.log tail
}]

[para]

For Docker's iptables firewall backend, use [const {DOCKER-USER}] if the
host's [const {FORWARD}] chain jumps there. Packets have already gone
through destination NAT when they reach [const {DOCKER-USER}], so the action
must match the container-side destination ports. This does not change the
source address being banned.

[para]

Check the configured ports and chains on the host:

[example {
docker ps --format '{{.Names}}\t{{.Ports}}'
for fw in iptables ip6tables; do
    printf '\n%s\n' "$fw"
    "$fw" -t nat -S DOCKER | grep -E -- '--dport (80|443)( |$)'
    "$fw" -S FORWARD
    "$fw" -S DOCKER-USER
done
}]

[para]

The verified OpenACS mapping was:

[list_begin definitions]
[def {Public port 80 to container port 8080}]
TCP over IPv4 and IPv6.
[def {Public port 443 to container port 8443}]
TCP over IPv4 and IPv6, and UDP over IPv4.
[list_end]

[para]

The jail action for that mapping is:

[example {
action = iptables-multiport[actname=naviserver-tcp, name=naviserver-tcp, chain=DOCKER-USER, port="8080,8443", protocol=tcp]
         iptables-multiport[actname=naviserver-udp, name=naviserver-udp, chain=DOCKER-USER, port=8443, protocol=udp]
}]

[para]

The stock UDP action can install a ban in both address families. On this
setup only IPv4 has a published HTTP/3 port. These stock actions scope the
ban by protocol and destination port across forwarded traffic. If other
containers share those destination ports and should remain reachable from a
banned IP, scope the jump further to the intended service.

[para]

Docker's native nftables backend uses a different forwarding rule layout. Do
not use this [const {DOCKER-USER}] example on that backend; use an nftables
action on the host's forwarding path. Likewise, if a host process terminates
the connections, use the host-local path or enforce the ban at the proxy.

[section {Enable and verify}]

The example jail starts disabled. To activate it, replace
[const {enabled = false}] with the following line under
[const {[naviserver-probes]}] in
[file /etc/fail2ban/jail.d/naviserver-probes.local]:

[example {
 enabled  = true
}]

[para]

Then check the configuration, reload Fail2Ban, and confirm the jail is
active. Run as root, or use sudo:

[example {
 sudo fail2ban-client -t && fail2ban-client reload && fail2ban-client status naviserver-probes
}]

[para]

The configuration check does not exercise the firewall. For a full test,
emit three fresh markers from the NaviServer console ([const {nscp}]), using
an address reserved for documentation:

[example {
 for {set i 0} {$i < 3} {incr i} {
    ns_log Notice "F2B_NAVISERVER_PROBE ip=192.0.2.11"
    if {$i < 2} {
        after 1100
    }
 }
}]

[para]

After a few seconds, list the currently banned IP addresses in this jail.
The output should include [const {192.0.2.11}]:

[example {
 sudo fail2ban-client get naviserver-probes banip
}]

[para]

While the ban is active, inspect the installed rules:

[example {
iptables -S DOCKER-USER    # use INPUT for the host-local setup
iptables -S f2b-naviserver-tcp
iptables -S f2b-naviserver-udp # omit if the UDP action is disabled
}]

[para]

The protocol/port jumps should precede the final [const {RETURN}] in [const {DOCKER-USER}], and the per-jail chains should show the banned source
address. Rules may be created on demand at the first ban.

[para]

Remove the test ban when you are done:

[example {
 sudo fail2ban-client set naviserver-probes unbanip 192.0.2.11
}]

[para]

For IPv6, repeat with [const {2001:db8::11}] and inspect [const {ip6tables}]. These tests verify log processing and firewall rule
installation without blocking a real visitor.

[para]

On OpenACS, the colored filter matched a real NaviServer log marker, manual
bans installed and removed IPv4 and IPv6 rules, and three console-generated
markers caused an automatic ban.

